⸻

⏱ 8–15 Minutes — What is a Spark DataFrame?

Now let’s upgrade.

A Spark DataFrame is distributed.

That means:
	•	Data is split into partitions
	•	Each partition runs as a task
	•	Tasks run in parallel across executors
	•	Executors run on a cluster

So instead of one machine processing data,
multiple machines work simultaneously.

That’s how Spark scales.

⸻

Why Spark DataFrames are powerful

They are:
	•	Schema-aware
	•	Optimized by Catalyst
	•	Executed using Tungsten engine
	•	Available in Python, Scala, SQL

But here’s the important concept:

Spark does not execute transformations immediately.

Which brings us to Lazy Evaluation.

⸻

⏱ 15–22 Minutes — Lazy Evaluation (Very Important)

This is the core Spark concept.

When you write:
	•	select()
	•	filter()
	•	join()
	•	groupBy()

Spark does NOT run it.

Instead:
It builds a logical plan.

Think of it as writing a recipe.

Spark collects all transformations into a DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph).

Execution happens only when an action is called.

Actions:
	•	show()
	•	count()
	•	collect()
	•	write()

Remember this sentence:

Transformations build the plan.
Actions trigger execution.

⸻

Why does Spark do this?

Because it wants to optimize the entire pipeline.

Before running, Spark can:
	•	Push filters down
	•	Remove unnecessary steps
	•	Choose better join strategies
	•	Combine stages

That’s why Spark is fast at scale.

